// Generated by tools/build_site.py from content/free-questions — free practice questions.
(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["ic-38"] = {"qs": [["Life Insurance & Financial Planning", "An emergency fund for meeting sudden expenses should preferably be kept in:", ["Liquid, low-risk instruments such as a savings account or a liquid fund", "Real estate, because property prices generally rise", "Long-term deposits that carry a heavy penalty for premature withdrawal before maturity", "Small-cap equity shares to maximise returns"], 0, "An emergency fund must be available quickly without loss of value, so liquidity and safety matter most. Property, locked-in deposits and volatile equities are unsuitable for this purpose."], ["Life Insurance Products", "A borrower wants life cover that reduces in line with his outstanding home loan. The most suitable product is:", ["A whole life policy", "An immediate annuity", "Mortgage redemption (decreasing term) assurance", "A money-back policy with survival benefits every five years"], 2, "Mortgage redemption assurance is a decreasing term plan whose sum assured falls roughly with the loan balance, so the loan can be cleared if the borrower dies. It is cheaper than a level term plan."], ["Pricing & Valuation", "Under the level premium system, the premiums paid in the early years of a policy are:", ["Less than the actual cost of risk, with the resulting deficit being recovered from the insurer's shareholders in later years", "Exactly equal to the cost of risk in each year", "Refunded to the policyholder at the end of each year", "More than the actual cost of risk, the excess building a reserve for later years"], 3, "With a level premium, the policyholder pays the same amount every year. In early years, this exceeds the cost of mortality; the surplus builds a reserve that meets the higher risk of later years."], ["Pricing & Valuation", "In a life insurer's participating fund, surplus arises mainly when:", ["More policyholders lapse their policies after one year", "The insurer raises its premium rates retrospectively", "Actual experience of mortality, interest and expenses is better than assumed", "Claims are delayed beyond the stipulated time"], 2, "Premiums are based on cautious assumptions. If fewer people die, investments earn more, or expenses are lower than assumed, a surplus emerges, part of which is distributed to participating policyholders as bonus."], ["Health Insurance", "AYUSH treatment covered under many health policies refers to:", ["Ayurveda, Yoga and Naturopathy, Unani, Siddha and Homoeopathy", "Allopathy, Yoga, Unani, Surgery and Homoeopathy", "Acupuncture, Yoga, Unani, Siddha and Hydrotherapy", "Ayurveda, Yoga, Unani, Sowa-Rigpa and Herbal remedies only"], 0, "AYUSH stands for Ayurveda, Yoga and Naturopathy, Unani, Siddha and Homoeopathy. Health policies cover in-patient treatment under these systems in recognised hospitals, subject to policy terms."], ["Life Insurance Claims", "Under a money-back policy, a survival benefit is paid:", ["Only when the insured dies during the term", "Only when the policy is surrendered", "Each year as a fixed percentage of the premium paid", "When the insured survives to specified dates during the policy term"], 3, "Money-back plans pay a portion of the sum assured at fixed intervals if the life assured is alive on those dates. These are survival benefits and are separate from the maturity and death benefits."], ["Life Insurance & Financial Planning", "If inflation averages 6% per annum, an annual household expense of ₹50,000 today will cost approximately how much after 12 years?", ["₹86,000", "₹89,542", "₹1,00,610", "₹1,06,646"], 2, "Future cost = 50,000 × (1.06)^12 ≈ ₹1,00,610. ₹86,000 wrongly applies simple growth (6% × 12 years), while ₹89,542 and ₹1,06,646 compound for 10 and 13 years respectively."], ["Introduction to Insurance", "Insurers can estimate their likely claims for the coming year with reasonable accuracy mainly because of:", ["The principle of subrogation applied to every claim", "The law of large numbers", "The fact that every insured person will definitely suffer a loss", "Government guarantees on all insurance losses"], 1, "When a large number of similar, independent exposures are pooled, actual losses tend to come close to expected losses. This law of large numbers lets insurers price risk and fix premiums sensibly."], ["Introduction to Insurance", "Which of the following is an example of the secondary burden of risk?", ["The cost of rebuilding a shop that has actually been destroyed by fire", "The value of stock stolen in a burglary at a godown", "The loss of a car that was written off in an accident", "Keeping large sums idle as a reserve to meet a possible future loss"], 3, "The primary burden is the actual loss suffered. The secondary burden includes costs borne even if no loss occurs, such as funds locked up as reserves and the fear and anxiety of uncertainty."], ["Introduction to Insurance", "In a fire policy covering a warehouse, the fire itself is best described as the:", ["Hazard", "Moral hazard", "Peril", "Risk premium"], 2, "A peril is the event that actually causes the loss, such as fire, flood or theft. A hazard is a condition that increases the chance or size of loss, like storing inflammable goods carelessly."], ["Life Insurance Products", "In a deferred annuity, the annuity payments:", ["Begin immediately, one period after the single premium is paid", "Begin after a chosen deferment period during which the purchase money accumulates", "Are paid only to the nominee after the annuitant's death, together with all the accumulated interest", "Are paid only once, as a lump sum on retirement"], 1, "A deferred annuity has an accumulation phase, after which income begins at the vesting date. In an immediate annuity, payments start straight away, from the end of the first payment period."], ["Pricing & Valuation", "Over a year, the total premium under the annual mode is usually lower than under the monthly mode because:", ["Monthly-mode policyholders have higher mortality", "Monthly premiums attract goods and services tax, whereas annual premiums are fully exempt from any such tax levy", "Annual-mode policies do not carry any insurance cover", "The insurer saves on collection costs and earns interest on the money for a longer period"], 3, "Collecting premium once a year costs less, and the insurer receives the full year's money in advance, earning interest on it. Hence annual mode is cheaper, and monthly or quarterly modes carry a loading."], ["Life Insurance & Financial Planning", "Rakesh has a home loan outstanding of ₹20 lakh, expects children's education costs of ₹15 lakh, and needs a family income corpus of ₹40 lakh. He already has life cover of ₹25 lakh and savings of ₹10 lakh. Using the needs approach, his additional life cover requirement is:", ["₹75 lakh", "₹50 lakh", "₹65 lakh", "₹40 lakh"], 3, "Total needs = 20 + 15 + 40 = ₹75 lakh. Less existing cover (₹25 lakh) and savings (₹10 lakh) gives a gap of ₹40 lakh. ₹75 lakh ignores existing resources; ₹50 lakh ignores savings."], ["Legal Principles of Insurance", "Which of the following would be a material fact for a life insurance proposal?", ["The proposer's favourite sport is cricket", "The proposer recently changed his mobile handset and telephone service provider", "The proposer prefers vegetarian food on weekdays", "The proposer underwent heart bypass surgery two years ago"], 3, "A material fact is one that would influence the underwriter's decision to accept the risk or the premium charged. A past heart surgery clearly affects mortality risk; the other facts are irrelevant."], ["Legal Principles of Insurance", "Who among the following has an insurable interest in the life of a person?", ["A close neighbour who has lived in the same housing society for many years", "A fan who admires that person's public work", "A colleague who works in another department", "A bank that has lent money to that person, to the extent of the loan"], 3, "Insurable interest exists where a person would suffer a financial loss from the death of the life assured. A creditor has such interest up to the debt amount; a neighbour, fan or colleague does not."], ["Health Insurance", "A policy allows room rent of ₹5,000 per day, but the insured chooses a room costing ₹10,000 per day. Associated medical expenses to which proportionate deduction applies are ₹80,000. The amount payable for these expenses is:", ["₹80,000", "₹60,000", "₹20,000", "₹40,000"], 3, "The eligible room is half the room taken (5,000 ÷ 10,000), so associated expenses are paid proportionately: 80,000 × 0.5 = ₹40,000. Items such as pharmacy and implants are excluded from proportionate deduction."], ["Regulatory Aspects of Agency", "A customer hands over the renewal premium in cash to his agent. As a matter of proper conduct, the agent should:", ["Keep it with him until the end of the grace period to earn interest", "Deposit it with the insurer promptly and ensure the customer gets the insurer's official receipt", "Give the customer a handwritten personal receipt on his own letterhead in place of the insurer's official premium receipt", "Adjust part of it against his own commission before depositing"], 1, "Premium belongs to the insurer and must be remitted promptly; only the insurer's receipt is valid proof of payment. Holding the money or deducting commission from it is a serious breach of conduct."], ["Life Insurance Products", "A simple reversionary bonus of ₹45 per thousand sum assured is declared on a policy with a sum assured of ₹4,00,000. The bonus added to the policy for that year is:", ["₹18,000", "₹1,800", "₹45,000", "₹9,000"], 0, "Bonus = 45 × (4,00,000 ÷ 1,000) = 45 × 400 = ₹18,000. Simple reversionary bonus is calculated on the basic sum assured only and is added to the policy, payable with the claim."], ["Life Insurance Claims", "If the life assured dies during the grace period without having paid the due premium, the insurer will usually:", ["Pay the claim after deducting the unpaid premium", "Reject the claim because the premium was unpaid", "Pay only the surrender value of the policy", "Pay only the premiums received so far"], 0, "During the grace period the policy remains in force. If death occurs then, the claim is admitted and the unpaid premium due is deducted from the claim amount."], ["Life Insurance & Financial Planning", "Before recommending any insurance product, an agent should first:", ["Show the product with the highest commission", "Understand the customer's goals, income, liabilities and existing cover", "Collect the first premium so that the customer does not change his mind", "Compare competitor products only on the basis of premium"], 1, "Need-based selling begins with fact-finding: goals, income, dependants, loans and current cover. Only after this analysis can a suitable product and sum assured be recommended."], ["Pricing & Valuation", "The tabular premium for a plan is ₹48 per thousand sum assured, and a rebate of ₹2 per thousand applies for the sum assured chosen. What is the annual premium for a sum assured of ₹5,00,000?", ["₹23,000", "₹24,000", "₹22,000", "₹23,500"], 0, "Net rate = 48 − 2 = ₹46 per thousand. Premium = 46 × 500 = ₹23,000. ₹24,000 ignores the rebate, while ₹22,000 deducts it twice."], ["Legal Principles of Insurance", "After paying a full own-damage claim, a motor insurer files a suit against the negligent truck owner who caused the accident to recover the amount. This right arises from the principle of:", ["Subrogation", "Contribution", "Utmost good faith", "Proximate cause"], 0, "Subrogation transfers the insured's rights against a third party to the insurer after it has paid the loss, preventing the insured from being paid twice. Contribution concerns sharing a loss between insurers."], ["Life Insurance Claims", "For settling a maturity claim, the insurer normally requires:", ["A death certificate issued by the local municipal authority", "A signed discharge voucher together with the original policy document and bank and KYC details", "A First Information Report lodged with the police, along with the post-mortem report and the police inquest report", "A fresh medical examination of the policyholder"], 1, "A maturity claim is paid to the policyholder on survival to the end of the term. The insurer needs a discharge form, the policy document and payment details; death documents or medicals are irrelevant here."], ["Health Insurance", "Day-care procedures in health insurance are:", ["Routine outpatient consultations with a general physician", "Treatments taken only at home on a doctor's advice", "Treatments that need less than 24 hours of hospitalisation because of advanced technology, and are covered", "Cosmetic procedures done in a clinic in a single day"], 2, "Procedures such as cataract surgery or chemotherapy can now be done in under 24 hours. Policies cover listed day-care procedures even though the usual 24-hour hospitalisation condition is not met."], ["Health Insurance", "A super top-up policy has a deductible of ₹3 lakh. During the policy year, the insured has two admissible hospitalisation claims of ₹2 lakh and ₹2.5 lakh. The super top-up insurer will pay:", ["₹1.5 lakh", "Nil", "₹4.5 lakh", "₹2 lakh"], 0, "Aggregate claims = 2 + 2.5 = ₹4.5 lakh. Excess over the ₹3 lakh deductible = ₹1.5 lakh. A regular top-up with a per-claim deductible would pay nil, as neither single claim exceeds ₹3 lakh."], ["Documentation & Underwriting", "Which of the following is most likely to make an underwriter ask for a detailed medical examination, even for a modest sum assured?", ["The proposer being a salaried employee of a government bank", "A history of diabetes and high blood pressure disclosed in the proposal", "The proposer choosing the annual mode of premium payment", "The proposer having submitted a passport as age proof"], 1, "Disclosed medical conditions such as diabetes or hypertension directly affect mortality, so the underwriter needs medical evidence to classify the life as standard or substandard. The other facts do not raise health concerns."], ["Introduction to Insurance", "A restaurant stores cylinders of cooking gas and drums of kerosene right next to its open kitchen fire. This condition is an example of:", ["Moral hazard", "Physical hazard", "Speculative risk", "Risk retention"], 1, "A physical hazard is a tangible condition of the property or its surroundings that increases the chance of loss. Moral hazard, in contrast, relates to the attitude or dishonesty of the insured person."], ["Health Insurance", "The cost of diagnostic tests done a few days before admission, for the same illness for which the insured is hospitalised, is covered under:", ["Domiciliary hospitalisation", "Pre-hospitalisation expenses", "Day-care treatment", "Post-hospitalisation expenses"], 1, "Pre-hospitalisation cover pays for related medical expenses incurred for a specified number of days before admission. Post-hospitalisation cover applies to expenses after discharge."], ["Pricing & Valuation", "The net premium of a life insurance policy is calculated mainly on the basis of:", ["Mortality, interest and agent's commission", "Expenses and expected bonus", "Mortality and interest", "Taxes and the insurer's profit target"], 2, "Net (pure) premium reflects only expected mortality cost discounted at an assumed rate of interest. Loadings for expenses, commission, contingencies and bonus are added later to arrive at the office premium."], ["Customer Service & Grievances", "Which of the following is a sign of positive body language when an agent meets a prospect?", ["Comfortable eye contact and an open, relaxed posture", "Arms folded tightly across the chest throughout the meeting", "Frequently checking the mobile phone while the prospect speaks", "Looking away and tapping fingers on the table"], 0, "Steady eye contact and an open posture show attention, honesty and confidence. Folded arms, checking the phone or fidgeting send signals of disinterest or defensiveness and weaken trust."], ["Life Insurance Products", "In a ULIP, an annual premium of ₹50,000 is paid. The premium allocation charge is 5% and the NAV on the date of allocation is ₹20. How many units are allotted?", ["2,500", "2,250", "2,425", "2,375"], 3, "Amount invested = 50,000 − 5% = ₹47,500. Units = 47,500 ÷ 20 = 2,375. 2,500 ignores the allocation charge, while 2,250 and 2,425 apply the charge incorrectly."], ["Life Insurance & Financial Planning", "A couple whose children are now financially independent, and who have about ten years left until retirement, should usually give priority to:", ["Buying the largest possible child education plan", "Taking a new long-tenure home loan to buy a second house for investment", "Investing all savings in a single high-risk small-cap stock", "Building a retirement corpus and ensuring adequate health cover"], 3, "In the 'empty nest' stage, family protection needs reduce while retirement and health needs grow. Building a retirement corpus and securing health cover are the main goals for this life stage."], ["Life Insurance & Financial Planning", "Over a long period, which of these asset classes typically carries the highest risk along with the highest expected return?", ["Equity shares", "Bank savings account", "Bank fixed deposit", "Government of India bonds held to maturity"], 0, "Equity offers higher expected long-term returns but with high volatility. Savings accounts, fixed deposits and government bonds held to maturity provide lower but more stable and predictable returns."], ["Regulatory Aspects of Agency", "An agent's commission on a policy is paid by:", ["The policyholder, as a separate fee over and above the premium", "IRDAI, from the fees collected from insurers", "The insurer, out of the premium it receives", "The Insurance Council, from its annual budget"], 2, "Agents are remunerated by the insurer, and the cost of commission is built into the premium loading. The policyholder does not pay a separate fee to the agent, and neither IRDAI nor the Council pays commission."], ["Pricing & Valuation", "Actuarial valuation of a life insurer is carried out mainly to:", ["Fix the commission of each agent", "Compare its liabilities with its assets and determine surplus or deficit", "Decide the premium for a single new policy", "Calculate the income tax payable by each policyholder on the maturity proceeds of his policy"], 1, "Valuation estimates the present value of future policy liabilities and compares it with assets. The resulting surplus, if any, helps decide bonus to participating policyholders and confirms solvency."], ["Documentation & Underwriting", "The First Premium Receipt (FPR) issued by a life insurer signifies that:", ["The proposal is still under consideration", "The insurance contract has commenced", "The policy has been assigned to a bank", "The free-look period has expired"], 1, "The FPR is issued once the proposal is accepted and the first premium is adjusted. It is evidence that the contract has started; subsequent premiums are acknowledged through renewal premium receipts."], ["Documentation & Underwriting", "Under Section 39 of the Insurance Act, a nomination can be made by:", ["A policyholder who has taken a policy on his or her own life", "Any person who pays the premium on someone else's life", "The nominee, on behalf of the policyholder", "The agent, at the time of collecting the proposal"], 0, "Nomination is permitted only where the policyholder has insured his or her own life. It names who will receive the claim money. It can be made at proposal stage or later, by an endorsement."], ["Regulatory Aspects of Agency", "Under Section 40 of the Insurance Act, 1938, an insurer is NOT permitted to pay commission or remuneration for procuring business to:", ["An individual agent appointed by the insurer", "A corporate agent registered with IRDAI", "An insurance broker licensed by IRDAI and acting on behalf of the client who placed the business", "A person who is not an agent or intermediary authorised under the Act"], 3, "Section 40 allows commission only to persons authorised to solicit insurance, such as appointed agents and licensed intermediaries. Paying an unlicensed person for bringing in business is prohibited."], ["Life Insurance Claims", "Within three years of the policy, a death claim may be repudiated mainly if the insurer can establish:", ["That the agent's licence had expired", "Fraud or suppression of material facts in the proposal", "That the nominee lives in another state", "That the claim was submitted within the prescribed time"], 1, "Under Section 45, a policy can be questioned within three years on grounds of fraud or misstatement or suppression of material facts. Administrative issues such as the agent's licence or nominee's address are not grounds."], ["Life Insurance Claims", "For an accidental death benefit claim, in addition to the usual death claim papers, the insurer generally requires:", ["A certificate from the employer only", "The original age proof of the nominee", "A fresh proposal form signed by the nominee", "The FIR, post-mortem report and police inquest report"], 3, "Since the benefit depends on death being accidental, the insurer needs police and medical records — FIR, inquest and post-mortem reports — to establish the cause and circumstances of death."], ["Pricing & Valuation", "Other things being equal, the premium for a term plan for a 45-year-old is higher than for a 25-year-old because:", ["The probability of death rises with age", "Older people are always charged a penalty by IRDAI", "The interest rate assumed is lower for older people", "Commission rates are higher for older proposers"], 0, "Mortality tables show the probability of death increasing with age. Since the cost of providing cover depends on this probability, the premium for an older life is higher."], ["Life Insurance Products", "A whole life policy pays the sum assured:", ["Only if the insured survives a fixed term of 20 years", "On the death of the insured whenever it occurs (or at a very high specified age)", "Only on death caused by an accident", "In equal instalments every five years during the term, with the balance paid on survival to maturity"], 1, "Whole life insurance provides cover for the entire lifetime, so the claim arises on death whenever it happens; many plans also pay at a high age such as 99 or 100."], ["Regulatory Aspects of Agency", "The Insurance Regulatory and Development Authority of India (IRDAI) was established under:", ["The Insurance Act, 1938", "The IRDA Act, 1999", "The LIC Act, 1956", "The General Insurance Business (Nationalisation) Act, 1972"], 1, "IRDAI is a statutory body created by the Insurance Regulatory and Development Authority Act, 1999. The Insurance Act, 1938 is the main law governing insurance business, which IRDAI administers."], ["Legal Principles of Insurance", "In the formation of an insurance contract, the 'offer' is usually made by:", ["The insurer, by publishing its sales brochure and advertisements", "The proposer, by submitting a signed proposal form", "The agent, by giving a premium quotation", "IRDAI, by approving the product"], 1, "A brochure or quotation is only an invitation to offer. The proposer makes the offer through the proposal form, and the insurer accepts it, often with or without modifications, by issuing the policy."], ["Legal Principles of Insurance", "A term in a policy that must be strictly complied with, whether or not it is material to the risk, and whose breach allows the insurer to avoid the policy, is a:", ["Warranty", "Representation", "Endorsement", "Recital clause"], 0, "A warranty is a promise by the insured that must be literally fulfilled. A breach entitles the insurer to avoid the contract even if the breach did not contribute to the loss. A representation only needs to be substantially true."], ["General Insurance", "Marine hull insurance covers:", ["The ship itself, including its machinery, against perils of the sea", "Only the cargo carried on the ship", "Only the wages of the ship's crew", "Goods carried by road from the port to the warehouse"], 0, "Marine insurance has two main branches: hull, covering the vessel and its machinery, and cargo, covering goods in transit. Inland road transit is covered under inland transit or cargo policies."], ["Customer Service & Grievances", "A policyholder who alleges deficiency in service by an insurer can file a complaint before a Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission under:", ["Section 41 of the Insurance Act, 1938", "The Indian Contract Act, 1872", "The Consumer Protection Act, 2019", "The Life Insurance Corporation Act, 1956"], 2, "Insurance is a 'service' under the Consumer Protection Act, 2019, so policyholders can approach District, State or National Commissions for deficiency in service. The other Acts do not set up consumer forums."], ["General Insurance", "Under a reinstatement value fire policy, a claim is settled on the basis of:", ["The cost of replacing the property with new property of the same kind, without deduction for depreciation", "The market value of the property after deducting depreciation", "The original purchase price of the property as shown in the books of account, regardless of when it was bought", "Half of the sum insured, irrespective of the actual loss"], 0, "Reinstatement value cover pays the cost of rebuilding or replacing with new property of the same kind and capacity, without a deduction for depreciation. A market value policy deducts depreciation."], ["Life Insurance Claims", "Which document is essential for any death claim under a life policy?", ["The maturity discharge voucher", "The original proposal form", "The death certificate", "The agent's licence copy"], 2, "A death claim cannot be settled without proof of death, normally the death certificate issued by the local authority, along with the claim form and the claimant's identity and bank details."], ["Customer Service & Grievances", "Active listening by an insurance agent mainly involves:", ["Talking continuously about the product's many features so that there is never any awkward silence in the meeting", "Preparing the next sales pitch while the customer is still speaking", "Agreeing with every statement of the customer to avoid any disagreement", "Paying full attention to the customer, clarifying doubts and responding to what is actually said"], 3, "Active listening means concentrating on the speaker, asking clarifying questions and responding to their real concerns. It builds trust and helps the agent understand the customer's needs before suggesting a product."], ["Introduction to Insurance", "Which of the following is an example of a pure risk?", ["The chance that a factory may be damaged by a flood", "Buying gold for trading in the hope of a quick profit", "Purchasing a lottery ticket that may win a prize", "Starting a new business that may earn a profit or make a loss"], 0, "A pure risk offers only the possibility of loss or no loss, as with flood damage. The other options are speculative risks, which carry a chance of gain as well, and are normally not insurable."], ["Health Insurance", "A family floater health policy provides:", ["A separate, equal sum insured for each family member, which cannot be shared with any other member", "Cover only for the eldest member of the family", "Cover for the family only while travelling abroad", "One sum insured that can be used by any one or all of the covered family members"], 3, "Under a floater, the sum insured 'floats' across the family: any member can use it, and total claims by all members cannot exceed it in the year. Individual policies give each person a separate sum insured."], ["Documentation & Underwriting", "Among these documents, which one would an insurer normally regard as weaker, non-standard evidence of the proposer's age?", ["A passport", "A birth certificate issued by the municipal registrar of births", "A school leaving certificate", "A self-declaration or affidavit of age"], 3, "Non-standard age proofs, such as an affidavit or self-declaration, are less reliable, and insurers may accept them only with restrictions or an extra premium. Birth certificates, passports and school certificates are standard proofs."], ["Documentation & Underwriting", "A proposer with a modest declared income asks for a very large sum assured without any apparent financial need. The underwriter's main concern here is:", ["The proposer's age proof being non-standard", "The premium mode chosen by the proposer", "Over-insurance and the possibility of moral hazard", "The agent's eligibility for commission"], 2, "Financial underwriting checks that the sum assured is reasonable relative to income and need. Excessive cover without justification suggests speculation or moral hazard, so the underwriter may limit the cover."], ["Life Insurance Products", "Term insurance with return of premium (TROP) differs from a pure term plan mainly because:", ["The premiums paid are returned if the insured survives the policy term", "It pays double the sum assured on death due to any cause during the policy term", "It does not require any medical underwriting", "It has no fixed policy term"], 0, "Under TROP, the total premiums paid are refunded on survival to maturity, while a pure term plan pays nothing on survival. For this extra benefit, TROP premiums are noticeably higher."], ["Legal Principles of Insurance", "A storm blows the roof off a warehouse and rain then enters and spoils the goods inside. The policy covers storm but excludes rain damage. The loss to the goods is:", ["Not payable, because rain was the last cause in time", "Payable only up to half, because two causes were involved", "Payable, because the storm is the proximate cause of the damage", "Not payable, because a warehouse cannot be insured against storm"], 2, "Proximate cause is the active, efficient cause that sets the chain of events in motion. The storm caused the rain to enter, so the storm is the proximate cause and the loss is covered."], ["Life Insurance & Financial Planning", "Life insurance is often called a unique asset because:", ["It always gives the highest return among all available investments, including equity shares and property", "Its value can never be affected by lapse or surrender", "It creates an immediate estate for the family as soon as the policy is in force", "It can be bought only by people with very high incomes"], 2, "Ordinary savings build up slowly, but a life policy guarantees the full sum assured to the family from the first premium if death occurs. This 'instant estate' is what makes life insurance unique."], ["Life Insurance Claims", "A policy has a sum assured of ₹5,00,000 and accrued bonus of ₹60,000. On the policyholder's death, there is an outstanding policy loan of ₹1,20,000 with unpaid interest of ₹8,000. The net claim payable is:", ["₹4,32,000", "₹4,40,000", "₹5,60,000", "₹3,72,000"], 0, "Claim = 5,00,000 + 60,000 − 1,20,000 − 8,000 = ₹4,32,000. ₹4,40,000 ignores the interest, and ₹5,60,000 ignores the loan altogether. The insurer recovers the loan and interest from the claim."], ["Customer Service & Grievances", "In the language of customer service, a 'moment of truth' refers to:", ["The day on which the insurer finally settles and pays a claim after completing all its investigations", "The time when the agent discloses his commission", "Any point of contact at which the customer forms an impression of the service", "The final signature on the proposal form"], 2, "A moment of truth is every interaction — a phone call, a visit, a receipt — where the customer judges the quality of service. A claim payment is one such moment, but not the only one."], ["Customer Service & Grievances", "An agent always delivers policy documents, premium reminders and claim forms exactly when he has promised. Which dimension of service quality does this mainly reflect?", ["Tangibles", "Reliability", "Empathy", "Assurance"], 1, "Reliability is the ability to perform the promised service dependably and accurately. Tangibles relate to physical appearance, empathy to individual care, and assurance to knowledge and courtesy that inspire confidence."], ["General Insurance", "A company wants protection against loss of money caused by the dishonesty of its cashier. The most suitable cover is:", ["Burglary insurance", "Fidelity guarantee insurance", "Money-in-transit insurance", "Public liability insurance"], 1, "Fidelity guarantee insurance indemnifies an employer for financial loss caused by fraud or dishonesty of employees. Burglary and money-in-transit covers deal with outsiders' theft, not employee dishonesty."], ["Regulatory Aspects of Agency", "Under the code of conduct, while soliciting business an agent must:", ["Keep the name of his insurer confidential until the policy is issued", "Promise a fixed bonus rate for all future years", "Identify himself and disclose the name of the insurer he represents", "Collect the proposal without explaining the product features"], 2, "The code of conduct requires an agent to disclose his identity and licence, the insurer he represents and, on request, his commission. He must explain product features honestly and must not promise future bonuses."], ["General Insurance", "Which of the following is typically covered under a standard fire and special perils policy?", ["Gradual wear and tear of machinery", "Loss due to war or invasion", "Theft without any fire or other insured event", "Damage caused by lightning"], 3, "Lightning is a named peril under the standard fire and special perils policy. Wear and tear, war and simple theft are excluded or need separate covers such as burglary insurance."], ["Regulatory Aspects of Agency", "Which of the following is the most likely ground for cancellation of an individual agent's appointment?", ["Being found to have suppressed material facts or committed fraud while soliciting business", "Selling more policies than the agent's annual target", "Recommending a pure term plan instead of a savings-oriented endowment plan to a young customer with dependants and a home loan", "Advising a customer to update his nomination after marriage"], 0, "An insurer may cancel an agent's appointment for misconduct such as fraud, misrepresentation or breach of the code of conduct. Meeting targets or giving suitable advice are good practices, not grounds for cancellation."], ["Customer Service & Grievances", "A customer telephones the agent, very angry because his claim has been delayed. The agent's best first response is to:", ["Tell the customer that claim delays are the insurer's fault, not his, and end the call", "Advise the customer to file a court case immediately", "Argue that the customer must have submitted wrong documents", "Listen patiently, acknowledge the problem and promise a specific follow-up"], 3, "An upset customer first needs to be heard. Acknowledging the problem and committing to a definite follow-up calms the situation and preserves the relationship; blaming others or arguing usually worsens the complaint."], ["Customer Service & Grievances", "Under the Insurance Ombudsman mechanism, an award passed by the Ombudsman is:", ["Binding on the insurer once the complainant accepts it, while the complainant may instead pursue other remedies", "Binding on the complainant but only advisory for the insurer", "Advisory for both parties, and enforceable against the insurer only after it has been separately approved and notified by IRDAI", "Binding on both parties, with no further remedy available to anyone"], 0, "The Ombudsman's award binds the insurer, which must comply within the prescribed time once the complainant accepts it. A complainant who is not satisfied is free to reject it and approach other forums such as a consumer commission."], ["Documentation & Underwriting", "A change in the policyholder's address on an existing life policy is usually recorded by means of:", ["An endorsement", "A fresh proposal form", "A new policy bond", "A revival quotation"], 0, "Endorsements are written amendments that record changes in the terms or details of a policy after issue, such as address, nomination or plan changes. They form part of the policy document."], ["General Insurance", "A comprehensive (package) motor policy covers:", ["Only third-party liability as required by law", "Only own damage to the vehicle, without any third-party cover", "Only personal accident of passengers", "Both own damage to the insured vehicle and third-party liability"], 3, "A package policy combines own-damage cover for the vehicle with the compulsory third-party liability cover. A 'liability only' policy covers just the third-party portion required by law."], ["Introduction to Insurance", "A homeowner insures his house because its destruction would leave him financially ruined. Which rule of risk management is he following?", ["Don't risk more than you can afford to lose", "Don't risk a lot for a little", "Consider the likely outcomes carefully", "Avoid every activity that carries any risk at all"], 0, "The rule of not risking more than you can afford to lose says that a loss which would cause financial ruin must be transferred. 'Don't risk a lot for a little' is about weighing premium against cover."], ["General Insurance", "A public liability policy protects the insured against:", ["Damage to the insured's own building from fire", "Loss of cash due to dishonesty of employees", "Legal liability to third parties for bodily injury or property damage arising from its premises or operations", "Fall in the market value of the insured's shares"], 2, "Liability insurance covers the insured's legal liability to others. A public liability policy pays compensation to members of the public injured or whose property is damaged due to the insured's premises or activities."], ["Regulatory Aspects of Agency", "If a proposer is unable to fill in the proposal form himself, the agent may help by:", ["Filling it as per the proposer's answers, explaining the contents, and obtaining the proposer's signature with a suitable declaration", "Filling and signing the form on the proposer's behalf without telling him the contents", "Leaving the medical questions blank so that underwriting is quicker", "Writing down the answers that he believes the underwriter is most likely to accept, even where the proposer actually said something different"], 0, "The proposal remains the proposer's statement. If someone else fills it, the contents must be explained to the proposer, who signs it, and a declaration to this effect is recorded. Altering or omitting answers is unethical."], ["Life Insurance Products", "An accidental death benefit rider attached to a life policy:", ["Replaces the base sum assured on natural death", "Refunds all premiums if no accident occurs", "Pays an additional sum if death results from an accident", "Covers hospitalisation expenses for any illness"], 2, "A rider is an add-on to the base policy. The accidental death benefit rider pays an extra amount, over the base sum assured, if death is caused by an accident within the conditions of the rider."], ["Documentation & Underwriting", "When a policyholder nominates his minor son, he should also name:", ["An assignee who will become the owner of the policy", "A guarantor who will continue paying the premium", "A trustee under the Married Women's Property Act, 1874, to hold the policy money for the minor", "An appointee to receive the claim money during the nominee's minority"], 3, "A minor cannot give a valid discharge for the claim. So the policyholder names an appointee, who receives the policy money on the minor's behalf if the claim arises before the nominee turns 18."], ["Regulatory Aspects of Agency", "An agent describes a market-linked ULIP to a retired person seeking guaranteed monthly income as 'a fixed deposit with free insurance'. This is:", ["Acceptable, because a ULIP does have an insurance element", "Acceptable, if the customer signs the proposal form", "Rebating under Section 41 of the Insurance Act", "Mis-selling through misrepresentation of the product"], 3, "Describing a market-linked product as a guaranteed deposit misrepresents its risk and does not suit the customer's need. This is mis-selling. Rebating, in contrast, means sharing commission or premium as an inducement."], ["Life Insurance Products", "In a participating (with-profits) life policy, the policyholder:", ["Bears the full investment risk through units", "Receives no benefit other than the guaranteed sum assured", "Must participate in the insurer's board meetings", "Shares in the insurer's surplus through bonuses"], 3, "Participating policies share in the surplus of the insurer's par fund, usually through reversionary or terminal bonuses. Non-participating policies offer only the guaranteed benefits, and ULIPs pass investment risk to the policyholder."], ["Legal Principles of Insurance", "A contract entered into because one party's consent was obtained by undue influence is:", ["Void from the very beginning and therefore unenforceable by either party", "Voidable at the option of the party whose consent was so obtained", "Valid and fully enforceable against both parties", "Illegal and punishable as a criminal offence"], 1, "Under the Indian Contract Act, consent caused by coercion, undue influence, fraud or misrepresentation is not free, and the contract becomes voidable at the option of the aggrieved party."], ["Introduction to Insurance", "An insurer that has accepted a very large refinery risk passes a part of it to another insurer under a separate contract, without involving the policyholder. This arrangement is:", ["Double insurance", "Contribution", "Reinsurance", "Assignment"], 2, "Reinsurance is insurance bought by an insurer to share a part of its own risk. The policyholder deals only with the original insurer. Double insurance is when the insured buys cover from two insurers for the same risk."], ["Health Insurance", "A critical illness policy typically pays:", ["Only the actual hospital bills, up to the sum insured", "A lump sum on diagnosis of a listed illness, irrespective of actual expenses", "A fixed daily allowance for each day spent in hospital, subject to a maximum number of days", "Only the cost of medicines after discharge"], 1, "Critical illness cover is a benefit policy. On diagnosis of a covered illness such as cancer or a heart attack, and survival of any stated period, it pays a fixed lump sum regardless of the bills incurred."], ["Legal Principles of Insurance", "In an insurance contract, the consideration given by the insured is the:", ["Sum assured", "Proposal form", "Premium", "Policy document"], 2, "Consideration is what each party gives in exchange. The insured pays the premium, and the insurer gives its promise to pay on the happening of the insured event. The sum assured is the insurer's promised payment."], ["Life Insurance & Financial Planning", "₹1,00,000 invested at 8% per annum compounded annually will grow in 3 years to approximately:", ["₹1,24,000", "₹1,25,971", "₹1,16,640", "₹1,27,450"], 1, "Maturity value = 1,00,000 × (1.08)^3 = ₹1,25,971. ₹1,24,000 is the simple-interest figure, and ₹1,16,640 is the value after only two years of compounding."], ["Life Insurance Claims", "If a policyholder dies without a valid nomination or assignment, the claim amount is generally paid to:", ["The agent who sold the policy, to be distributed later", "The insurer's unclaimed amounts fund, immediately", "The legal heirs, on production of evidence of title such as a succession certificate", "The policyholder's employer"], 2, "Without a nominee or assignee, the insurer pays the persons legally entitled to the estate. It asks for evidence of title, such as a succession certificate or legal heir certificate, to obtain a valid discharge."], ["Health Insurance", "A super top-up health policy differs from a regular top-up policy because its deductible applies to:", ["Each separate hospitalisation claim individually", "Only claims made in the first year", "The total of all admissible claims in a policy year", "Only the pre- and post-hospitalisation expenses"], 2, "A regular top-up pays only when a single claim exceeds the deductible. A super top-up considers the aggregate of claims in the year, paying once the combined amount crosses the deductible."], ["Customer Service & Grievances", "Why is good service especially important in selling insurance compared with selling most physical goods?", ["Insurance products are identical across all insurers, so price is irrelevant", "Insurance is an intangible promise, so the service experienced is what the customer actually judges", "IRDAI fixes exactly the same premium for every policy of every insurer, so the only thing left to compete on is service", "Customers buy insurance only once in their lifetime and never renew it"], 1, "A customer cannot see or touch an insurance policy; he experiences it only through the service he receives and the claim at the end. Strong service builds trust, renewals and referrals."], ["Documentation & Underwriting", "The prospectus or sales brochure of a life insurance product must contain:", ["Only the insurer's past bonus rates and claim figures", "Only the name and contact details of the agent", "The product's features, benefits, exclusions and main terms and conditions", "A guarantee that the product will always outperform bank fixed deposits over every policy term"], 2, "The prospectus is meant to help a prospect make an informed decision, so it must describe the scope of benefits, conditions and exclusions honestly. It cannot promise returns that are not guaranteed."], ["Pricing & Valuation", "Life insurers often give a rebate in premium for a higher sum assured because:", ["Larger policies have no mortality risk", "Administrative costs per policy do not rise in proportion to the sum assured", "IRDAI requires every insurer to give a fixed discount on all policies with a sum assured above ₹1 lakh", "Agents are not paid any commission on large policies"], 1, "Many policy costs, such as issuing documents and maintaining records, are similar for small and large policies. Spread over a bigger sum assured, the expense per thousand falls, and the saving is passed on as a rebate."], ["Introduction to Insurance", "A large company running hundreds of delivery vans decides to pay for minor dents and scratches out of its own funds instead of insuring them. This is:", ["Risk transfer", "Risk avoidance", "Risk reduction through loss prevention", "Risk retention"], 3, "When an organisation consciously bears small, predictable losses itself, it is retaining the risk. Transfer would mean buying insurance, while avoidance would mean not running the vans at all."], ["General Insurance", "A standard burglary policy usually requires, for a claim to be admissible:", ["That the theft was committed by an employee of the insured", "Forcible and violent entry into or exit from the insured premises", "That the stolen goods were kept outside the premises", "That the loss occurred during a fire at the premises"], 1, "Burglary insurance covers theft involving forcible and violent entry or exit. Theft by employees is excluded (fidelity guarantee covers it), and goods outside the premises are generally not covered."], ["General Insurance", "A private car's own-damage loss is assessed at ₹38,000. The damaged parts, retained by the insured, have a salvage value of ₹2,000, and the compulsory deductible is ₹1,000. The claim payable is:", ["₹37,000", "₹36,000", "₹35,000", "₹38,000"], 2, "Claim = 38,000 − 2,000 (salvage retained by insured) − 1,000 (compulsory deductible) = ₹35,000. Paying ₹36,000 or ₹37,000 would ignore one of the deductions, giving the insured more than an indemnity."]]};
